1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,369
Hello, fellow aliens!

2
00:00:01,783 --> 00:00:04,354
Welcome to the fifth episode of Earthlings 101.

3
00:00:04,824 --> 00:00:08,044
Today we will talk about games, stories and dreams.

4
00:00:42,784 --> 00:00:48,212
As mentioned in the first episode, earthlings don't have the tools and instincts animals have to survive.

5
00:00:48,656 --> 00:00:52,745
Instead, nature gave them a brain and the capacity to learn and to create tools.

6
00:00:53,274 --> 00:00:57,918
Now, learning to survive, is kind of problematic, because you can't learn from experience.

7
00:00:58,320 --> 00:01:03,385
As soon as you experience how not to survive, you may be smarter, but you are also dead.

8
00:01:03,967 --> 00:01:07,167
The solution evolution found was something called imagination.

9
00:01:07,827 --> 00:01:13,442
Basically, earthlings create a simplified model of reality in their head and use it to predict the outcome of different actions.

10
00:01:13,949 --> 00:01:18,028
It's kind of a reality simulator, and it's very flexible, powerful and fast.

11
00:01:18,692 --> 00:01:20,992
However, sometimes it's not fast enough.

12
00:01:21,292 --> 00:01:28,012
When the earthling is in immediate danger, for example, there is no time to imagine - the earthling has to decide immediately or die.

13
00:01:28,512 --> 00:01:34,483
So, it would be great to be prepared for all kinds of dangerous situations, by some kind of training exercise.

14
00:01:34,984 --> 00:01:42,004
Earthlings have actually developed three mechanisms which might serve for exactly that purpose: Dreams, games, and stories.

15
00:01:42,684 --> 00:01:45,404
Dreams happen usually at night, during sleep.

16
00:01:45,651 --> 00:01:47,451
Earthlings are used to sleep every night.

17
00:01:47,911 --> 00:01:52,505
Sleeping means putting the body at rest and shutting down both voluntary movements and sensory system.

18
00:01:52,932 --> 00:01:57,497
Nobody knows the purpose of sleep, but sure is that the brain uses a part of this time to dream.

19
00:01:57,739 --> 00:02:03,058
Dreams are somewhat similar to those propaganda implants used in the Union of Galactic Communist Republics.

20
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:09,535
A propaganda implant activates itself during torpidity or hibernation and creates interactive propaganda hallucinations.

21
00:02:09,857 --> 00:02:16,160
To do so, it taps into both the sensory input and the motory output to make the brain believe that the hallucination is actually happening.

22
00:02:16,362 --> 00:02:19,867
Dreams are more or less the same, but without the propaganda implant.

23
00:02:20,050 --> 00:02:28,623
They are somewhat chaotic simulations of physical danger, social conflicts and other critical situations, created by the beast and thrown at the ego during sleep.

24
00:02:28,803 --> 00:02:31,852
Dreams are typically random series of threatening situations.

25
00:02:32,227 --> 00:02:48,807
For example, the dreamer runs down a hallway because he is late for work, and he is pursued by a predator, then he suddenly falls down a trap and finds himself unprepared in a final school exam, during a zombie attack, and the examiner is his mother, and everything happens on a sinking ship, and then he realizes that he is naked.

26
00:02:49,867 --> 00:02:55,447
All those situations are either physical or social danger situations, mixed randomly together by the beast.

27
00:02:55,847 --> 00:03:05,067
They may be actual concerns like being late for work, past fears like his mother or his final exam, as well as fears dating back to prehistoric times like being chased by a predator.

28
00:03:05,827 --> 00:03:10,053
The actual problems occurring in dreams can say a lot about the dreamer's unconscious fears.

29
00:03:10,717 --> 00:03:21,273
Human brain-doctors are trained to question patients about their dreams, to analyze the underlying fears, and then to come invariantly to the conclusion that the patient wants to kill his father and to have sex with his mother.

30
00:03:21,674 --> 00:03:23,034
This is called psychiatry.

31
00:03:24,434 --> 00:03:30,297
Scientific advice: When you abduct humans to experiment on them, you don't need an expensive memory erasing machine.

32
00:03:30,819 --> 00:03:34,312
Simply abduct humans in their sleep and put them back into their beds when you are done.

33
00:03:34,736 --> 00:03:40,110
Even if they remember the experiments, they will probably believe that it was just a bad dream, and forget it.

34
00:03:40,492 --> 00:03:43,435
The second survival training mechanism is called games.

35
00:03:43,942 --> 00:03:45,441
Doing a game is called playing.

36
00:03:45,794 --> 00:03:48,899
Like dreams, games are interactive danger simulations.

37
00:03:49,287 --> 00:03:56,361
But unlike dreams, games are played when the earthlings are awake, they can be shared, and the earthlings know that there is no real danger.

38
00:03:56,783 --> 00:04:04,464
Games are usually based on a set of more or less strict rules which fix the playing ground, the equipment, the allowed actions and the conditions for winning the game.

39
00:04:04,825 --> 00:04:09,785
Games are also full of symbols and rituals - something we will learn about in a forthcoming episode.

40
00:04:10,585 --> 00:04:17,332
There are many different kinds of games, but they are all made of four basic ingredients: Challenge, chance, make-believe and vertigo.

41
00:04:18,013 --> 00:04:22,955
Challenge means that the players compete with each other, or with the game itself, to maximize their score.

42
00:04:23,498 --> 00:04:29,252
This usually means to outrun, outjump, outsmart or out-whatever the other players, or the game itself.

43
00:04:29,252 --> 00:04:33,137
This is the game variant of the evolutionary principle "survival of the fittest".

44
00:04:33,559 --> 00:04:36,635
Chance means that many games include some kind of random elements.

45
00:04:36,936 --> 00:04:41,717
In most cases, the outcome is not completely random, as chance is combined with challenge.

46
00:04:42,159 --> 00:04:45,804
Those games are essentially training exercises for dealing with random events.

47
00:04:46,047 --> 00:04:47,332
The third ingredient is make-believe.

48
00:04:48,202 --> 00:04:53,222
The players pretend to be somebody else, and the game equipment symbolizes things in real life.

49
00:04:53,562 --> 00:04:57,711
This allows simulating all kinds of situations without need for realistic equipment.

50
00:04:58,197 --> 00:05:09,452
Earthling imagination can transform cardboard boxes into castles and ships, a house plant into a weapon of mass destruction, sticks and brooms into swords and lances, and a living room into the theater of epic battles.

51
00:05:10,093 --> 00:05:13,939
The fourth, somewhat surprising element of games is vertigo.

52
00:05:14,162 --> 00:05:18,527
This means disrupting the stability of perception to create some kind of voluptuous panic.

53
00:05:18,913 --> 00:05:24,911
The disruption can come from high altitude, the thrill of speed, spinning around, horror, or other factors.

54
00:05:25,192 --> 00:05:29,912
The evolutionary goal is possibly to train the earthling to keep a clear mind in stress situations.

55
00:05:30,532 --> 00:05:38,242
Vertigo in the larger sense includes other forms of dizziness, such as the thrill of high stakes, blood lust, drunkenness or even sexual arousal.

56
00:05:38,545 --> 00:05:43,788
Not all games feature all four ingredients, challenge, chance, make-believe and vertigo.

57
00:05:44,089 --> 00:05:46,769
But every game involves at least one of those ingredients.

58
00:05:47,989 --> 00:05:51,522
A popular kind of games are physical games, also known as sports.

59
00:05:51,890 --> 00:05:58,190
They involve, for example, sprinting, running after objects in teams, throwing things, and combat.

60
00:05:58,510 --> 00:06:04,055
Most sports are rather obvious training exercises for melee combat, hunting, and running from predators.

61
00:06:04,415 --> 00:06:14,572
The funny thing is that all these abilities are somewhat outdated, as modern earthlings buy their meat at the butcher, fight with firearms and have locked away all kinds of dangerous creatures into zoos.

62
00:06:14,773 --> 00:06:16,773
Except for the microbes.

63
00:06:19,793 --> 00:06:21,133
Tips for tourists.

64
00:06:21,833 --> 00:06:25,723
If you want to observe the variety of games, visit an amusement park.

65
00:06:26,027 --> 00:06:32,427
Here you'll find it all: Challenge and chance, make-believe, and huge machines uniquely dedicated to vertigo.

66
00:06:34,667 --> 00:06:45,151
For the alien observer, games look unpleasant - like forced training exercises for mercenary slaves: Combat training, tactical exercises, centrifuge training and so on.

67
00:06:45,411 --> 00:06:50,766
But the difference is that earthlings actually like playing, as their brains are programmed to enjoy games.

68
00:06:51,450 --> 00:06:53,508
Now to the third mechanism: Stories.

69
00:06:54,081 --> 00:06:58,238
In a nutshell, stories are made-up accounts on people in dangerous situations.

70
00:06:58,803 --> 00:07:05,107
Unlike dreams and games, stories are not interactive, but they make the audience think: "What would I do in this situation?".

71
00:07:05,589 --> 00:07:13,309
Stories are transmitted in various ways: They are told, played on stage, sung, written, drawn or filmed - to name just some media.

72
00:07:13,872 --> 00:07:16,662
Stories are often passed on from generation to generation.

73
00:07:17,066 --> 00:07:23,126
Some famous stories are thousands of years old and were transmitted through various media and translated into many languages.

74
00:07:24,046 --> 00:07:26,114
Many stories share a common structure.

75
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:29,903
Earthlings call this structure "the hero's journey", or "the monomyth".

76
00:07:30,290 --> 00:07:38,681
It goes like this: An earthling, called "hero", lives his ordinary life, when he is suddenly called to adventure - often in form of a mighty enemy threatening his world.

77
00:07:38,942 --> 00:07:43,136
He first refuses the call, but after a bit of encouragement, he accepts it.

78
00:07:43,277 --> 00:07:51,727
He meets a mentor who gives him advice and useful artefacts, and then enters an unknown world, encounters foes, passes trials and finds allies.

79
00:07:52,348 --> 00:07:57,747
Eventually he intrudes into the enemy's lair, gets into the heart of the lair, faces the enemy and defeats him.

80
00:07:58,088 --> 00:08:02,809
Then he gets the enemy's treasure and makes his way back to his world, often chased by foes.

81
00:08:03,031 --> 00:08:11,002
At the end of the journey, he lives a final moment of danger, death and rebirth, and finally returns home, richer than before and master of the two worlds.

82
00:08:12,086 --> 00:08:15,166
Not all stories stick strictly to this structure, though.

83
00:08:15,586 --> 00:08:20,169
There are, for example, stories called tragedies where the hero ultimately fails.

84
00:08:20,613 --> 00:08:23,735
But there is one point all stories have in common: Conflict.

85
00:08:24,262 --> 00:08:27,662
So, odds are that preparing for conflicts is the whole point of stories.

86
00:08:30,462 --> 00:08:31,424
Strategic advice.

87
00:08:31,827 --> 00:08:37,795
If you are planning to attack earth, with a giant bioengineered dinosaur, stay clear from an island called Japan.

88
00:08:38,295 --> 00:08:45,150
Japanese stories have prepared the Japanese for exactly this kind of attack since half a century, so they know to defend against such a monster.

89
00:08:45,554 --> 00:08:49,002
Better attack America, they are only prepared against zombie attacks.

90
00:08:49,509 --> 00:08:59,533
Besides being instructions for dangerous situations, stories have another, deeper aspect: Most of them feature myths and archetypes, ancient ideas deeply anchored in the human mind.

91
00:08:59,934 --> 00:09:02,112
We will learn about myths in another episode.

92
00:09:02,536 --> 00:09:09,082
Earthling cubs, also called children, spend most of their time sleeping, playing games, and listening to stories.

93
00:09:09,605 --> 00:09:13,454
Childhood is a phase of life where earthlings are still learning how to get along in the world.

94
00:09:13,881 --> 00:09:18,406
This is another hint that the purpose of dreams, stories and games might be actually: learning.

95
00:09:19,036 --> 00:09:23,175
Anthropologists have a lot of theories about the origin and purpose of games, dreams and stories.

96
00:09:23,481 --> 00:09:27,568
But fact is, all three mechanisms are all about dangerous situations.

97
00:09:28,069 --> 00:09:37,429
So, when an earthling encounters a dangerous situation, odds are that he already experienced this kind of situation in a dream, a game or a story - so he will know what to do.

98
00:09:37,689 --> 00:09:44,489
So it's not that far fetched to hypothesize that games, dreams and stories have all three developed for the same purpose: Learning to survive.

99
00:09:44,769 --> 00:09:49,830
So, once again, it all comes down to the most powerful force on earth, the genetic imperative.

100
00:09:50,252 --> 00:09:56,028
As a rule of thumb, whenever earthlings enjoy something, you can be pretty sure that the genetic imperative is behind it.

101
00:09:56,310 --> 00:10:02,442
In the next episode we will learn about aesthetics: What is beauty, what is art, and why are humans so crazy about it.

102
00:10:02,442 --> 00:10:07,519
Thanks for watching, and, as they say on Betelgeuse: Don't forget to be alien!

